==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/bɪd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-bid.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪd|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*gʷʰedʰ-}}
From {{der|en|enm|bidden}}, from {{der|en|ang|-}} {{m|ang|biddan||to ask, demand}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*bidjaną||to ask}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*gʷʰedʰ-}}. Conflated with {{der|en|ang|-}} {{m|ang|bēodan||to offer, announce}} (see Etymology 2 below). Compare {{cog|fy|bidde}}, {{cog|nds|bidden}}, {{cog|nl|bidden}} ("to pray"), {{cog|de|bitten}}, {{cog|da|bede}}, {{cog|nb|be}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|bids|bidding|bid|past2=bade|past3=bad|past_ptc2=bidden}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To issue a [[command]]; to [[tell]].
#: {{ux|en|He '''bade''' me come in.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|II|V|text=''Shylock'': [...] Why Jessica, I say!<br>''Launcelot'': Why, Jessica!<br>''Shylock'': Who '''bids''' thee call? I do not '''bid''' thee call.<br>''Launcelot'': Your worship was [[wont]] to tell me that I could do nothing without '''bidding'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[invite]]; to [[summon]].
#: {{ux|en|She was '''bidden''' to the wedding.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|II|V|text=''Jessica'': Call you? What is your will?<br>''Shylock'': I am '''bid''' forth to supper, Jessica: / [...] But [[wherefore]] should I go? / I am not '''bid''' for love; they flatter me;}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[utter]] a [[greeting]] or [[salutation]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|I|III|text=''Portia'': If I could '''bid''' the fifth welcome with so good heart as I / can '''bid''' the other four farewell, I should be glad of his / approach; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1960|month=November|author=L. Hyland|title=The Irish Scene|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=691|text=The last train&mdash;a three-coach A.E.C. unit&mdash;from Belfast to Crumlin and back, was '''bade''' farewell with fog signals as it carried a capacity crowd of last-trip travellers.}}

=====Usage notes=====
The inflected forms {{m|en|bade}}, {{m|en|bad}}{{,}} and {{m|en|bidden}} are becoming less common (outside certain set phrases like “bade farewell”) than uninflected ''bid''.<ref>“[http://grammarist.com/usage/bid-bade-bidden/ Bid, bade, bidden]”, ''Grammarist''</ref>

