===Etymology 2===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰewdʰ-}}
From {{der|en|enm|beden}}, from {{der|en|ang|-}} {{m|ang|bēodan||to offer, announce}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*beudaną||to offer}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰewdʰ-||be awake, aware}}. Conflated with {{der|en|ang|-}} {{m|ang|biddan||to ask, demand}} (see Etymology 1 above). Compare {{cog|nds|beden}}, {{cog|nl|bieden}}, {{cog|de|bieten}}, {{cog|da|byde}}, {{cog|nb|by}}. More at [[bede]].

====Verb====
{{en-verb|bids|bidding|bid}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To make an [[offer]] to [[pay]] or [[accept]] a certain [[price]].
#: {{ux|en|Have you ever '''bid''' in an auction?}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[offer]] as a [[price]].
#: {{ux|en|She '''bid''' £2000 for the Persian carpet.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To make an attempt.
#: {{ux|en|He was '''bidding''' for the chance to coach his team to victory once again.}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive|card games}} To [[announce]] (one's [[goal]]), before starting [[play]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[proclaim]] (a bede, prayer); to [[pray]].
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|I|x|passage=All night she spent in '''bidding''' of her bedes, / And all the day in doing good and godly deedes.}}

